The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not merely blow; it screamed. It tore at the stone walls of the lighthouse, rattling the brass fittings and shaking the dust from the high shelves. For Elias Thorne, this was the soundtrack of his life. For thirty years, he had been the custodian of the Great Beacon, a solitary figure in a world of gray waves and white foam. His days were measured in the turning of the lens and the changing of the tides, his nights in the rhythmic pulse of the light.

Elias was a man of few words and fewer friends. The supply boat came once a month, dropping off canned goods, oil, and a stack of newspapers that were already stale by the time they arrived. He lived in a state of comfortable isolation, his only companions the creaking of the tower and the distant, mournful cry of gulls.

It was on a Tuesday, when the sky was the color of bruised iron, that the letter arrived.

It was not in the usual crate of supplies. It was tucked into the crevice of the doorframe, a small, cream-colored envelope that looked absurdly delicate against the rough, salt-encrusted wood. There was no return
